<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Göttingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Georg-August-Universität Göttingen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, officially the Georg-August-Universität, is a public university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1734 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-great-britain">George II of Great Britain</a> as Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and opened in 1737. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> foundation with a large library and freedom of teaching, it became a leading European centre of scholarship, associated with figures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a> and the mathematician Gauss.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen.jpg" alt="University of Göttingen" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uni_G%C3%B6ttingen_Siegel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1734 George II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> ordered Gerlach Adolph von Münchhausen, a member of the privy council of the Electorate of Hanover, to establish a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideals. It began admitting students in 1737. The first infrastructure was modest, a riding hall and a fencing house, with lectures held in the Paulinerkirche, a Dominican monastery or professors' homes, and no university auditorium stood until the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> is reported to have said that Göttingen belongs neither to a state nor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and is the university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> was known among German universities for its free spirit and scientific exploration. Georg Christoph Lichtenberg held one of the first German professorships in experimental physics from 1769 to 1799, and by 1812 the library held over 200,000 volumes. It became known first for its Faculty of Law, where Johann Stephan Pütter taught public law for half a century, and by the centenary in 1837 law students often made up more than half of the student body. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a> taught there and compiled the first German dictionary. In 1837 seven professors, among them the Brothers Grimm, were expelled for protesting against the revocation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>'s liberal constitution of 1833 by King Ernest Augustus.</p><p>Around the turn of the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> reached its academic peak in mathematics and physics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-friedrich-gauss">Carl Friedrich Gauss</a> began the tradition, followed by Dirichlet and Riemann, and by 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hilbert">David Hilbert</a> and Felix Klein had made it a leading centre of mathematics. Ludwig Prandtl developed fluid mechanics and aerodynamics there from 1904, and Max Born led the theory group from 1921 to 1933, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a> was developed. American students such as Edward Everett and George Bancroft studied there. In the purge of 1933 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitlers-rise-to-power">Adolf Hitler's rise to power</a>, many professors, including Born, James Franck, Emmy Noether and Richard Courant, were dismissed or fled. The university reopened in 1945 under British control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Göttingen State and University Library, with around 9 million media units, is among the largest libraries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The university counts 47 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> winners among its affiliates by its own count. A tradition holds that new doctors are driven in a decorated wagon to the Marktplatz, where they kiss the statue of the Gänseliesel, the goose girl of a fairy tale. An old inscription on the Ratskeller wall reads Extra Gottingam non est vita, which means there is no life outside Göttingen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Göttingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
